      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the warmth and the low murmur of conversation. Rain meets your face\u2014a thin, persistent drizzle that beads on your collar and runs in silver threads down the cobbles.\n\nThe street is empty. Shuttered windows stare blind at the grey morning.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands at the end of the lane, perhaps sixty paces ahead. Even from here, you can feel the wrongness of it\u2014a cracked black arch rising against the pale sky like a wound that never healed. The stone is dark with moisture, the edges worn smooth by weather and time. Beyond it, nothing but mist and the suggestion of a road swallowed by fog.\n\nAs you approach, details resolve. The arch is perhaps twelve feet high, eight wide. Iron bands\u2014rusted, pitted\u2014brace the stone at intervals. Where a gate or door might once have hung, there's nothing but a solid wall of grey stone, fitted so precisely that the seams are almost invisible. But near the right edge, at waist height, you spot it: a recessed mechanism, a housing for a lock. The face of it has been filed flat\u2014deliberately, recently. Fresh scratches in the old iron catch the light.\n\nAnd at the foot of the arch, half-hidden in the wet gravel, something gleams.\n\nA single tarnished copper coin."
    },
