      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door groans open, and the warmth hits you again\u2014woodsmoke, spilled ale, damp wool. A few heads turn, then look away. The morning crowd is thin: a man nursing a cup by the window, two women in workman's coats sharing quiet words at the bar, Toran Vex still at his post with a glass he's been polishing since you left.\n\nYour old table stands in the corner. The one by the copper lamp with the dented shade, where the scarred wood holds the ring-marks of a hundred cups before yours.\n\nYou sit. The chair creaks. The rain ticks against the shutters.\n\nMara Venn is at a side table, papers spread before her\u2014accounts, by the look of them, a ledger open to a cramped column of figures. She glances up as you settle, holds your gaze for a beat too long, then returns to her work without a word.\n\nA few moments pass. The door opens.\n\nSable Quill steps in, shaking rain from his travel-stained cloak. He scans the room, nods once at Toran Vex, and crosses to the bar without removing his hood. He orders something low\u2014ale, by the gesture\u2014and leans against the counter, back to the room."
